# Metal–Organic Frameworks Incorporating Copper-Complexed Rotaxanes

## abstract
MOFs on the move: A copper coordinated [2]pseudorotaxanate which reacts with zinc nitrate to form an extended structure, consisting of three-fold interpenetrated networks, retains most of its solution-state chemistry including its ability to undergo electronic switching of some of the copper(I) ions under redox control.

Supporting information for this article is available on the WWW under http://dx.doi.org/10.1002/anie.201107873.
report the successful incorporation (Scheme 1) of copper(I)- complexed [2]pseudorotaxanate struts into a MOF (MOF1040). Each of the struts is composed of a rigid rod-like fragment containing a 1,10-phenanthroline unit which is encircled by a coordinating 30-membered ring. We present 1) the synthesis and solid-state structure of the strut in the form of a [2]pseudorotaxanate, 2) the preparation of MOF1040 and its single crystal structure, along with 3) data supporting the oxidation of copper(I) in the struts to copper(II), as well as 4) the demetalation of the strut, creating rotaxanes with free coordination sites within the solid-state structure of MOF-1040.
The synthesis of the copper-coordinated pseudorotaxanate $[\mathrm{\mathbf{1.Cu}]\cdot P F_{6}}$ and the subsequent preparation of MOF-1040 employing this strut are summarized in Scheme 1. The [2]pseudorotaxanate $[\mathrm{\mathbf{1}}.\mathrm{Cu}]{\cdot}\mathrm{PF}_{6}$ was prepared through the effective $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ -templated “gathering-and-threading” approach[5a,b] of a copper(I) catenate by 1) mixing of $\mathbf{2\cdotHCl^{[6]}}$ with $\mathrm{Et}_{3}\mathrm{N}$ , thus generating the dicarboxylate of 2, followed by 2) the addition of the copper complex[7] of 3, which was prepared by 3) the reaction of the coordinating ring with $\mathrm{[Cu(CH_{3}C N)_{4}]P F_{6}}$ in stoichiometric amounts, followed by 4) reprotonation of the dicarboxylate and counterion exchange with saturated aqueous $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{PF}_{6}$ solution, resulting in the production of $[\mathrm{\mathbf{1.Cu}]\cdot P F_{6}}$ in $87\%$ yield.
Dark red single crystals of the copper-coordinated [2]pseudorotaxanate were grown by diffusing $\mathrm{Et}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ into a solution of $[\mathbf{1}{\cdot}\mathrm{Cu}]{\cdot}\mathrm{PF}_{6}$ in $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ . The solid-state superstructure[8] (Figure 1), which occupies the triclinic space group $P\bar{1}$ , comprises three independent [2]pseudorotaxanates, in addition to disordered $\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{-}$ ions and $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ solvent molecules. The $\mathrm{{Cu-N}}$ bond lengths are typical (2.014 to $2.080\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\mathring{\cdot}$ ) of such complexes, and, while the ${\bf N-C u-N}$ angles are in some cases distorted significantly from ideal tetrahedral geometry (81 to $139^{\circ}$ ), this occurrence is in agreement with previously reported examples.[9] It would appear that these distortions occur to enable more efficient packing, as well as stabilizing intramolecular interactions, e.g., one of the phenoxy moieties of the ring component participates in $[\pi^{\cdots}\pi]$ stacking interactions with the phenanthroline ring system of the rod component. The three independent [2]pseudorotaxanates found in the unit cell arise primarily from the changes in the arrangement of the two phenanthroline ring systems and the intramolecular interactions they seek to satisfy.
Mixing $[\mathrm{\mathbf{1.Cu}]\cdot P F_{6}}$ and $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ in diethylformamide (DEF) at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a sealed tube for $48\mathrm{{h}}$ resulted (see Experimental Section) in the formation of red cubic crystals of MOF-1040. The single-crystal $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray diffraction study was performed using synchrotron radiation in the beamline 24- ID-C at NECAT, in the Advanced Photon Source (APS) at Argonne National Laboratory. The crystal structure was solved in the monoclinic space group $P2_{1}/c$ ,[10] revealing a structure (Figure 2) consisting of threefold interpenetrated networks. The inorganic $\mathrm{{Zn_{4}O}}$ secondary building units (SBUs) are linked by the [1·Cu] complex struts, producing a framework with pcu topology.[11] The inorganic SBUs, the Cu complexes and the rest of the aromatic rings in the organic strut were unambiguously determined from the single-crystal structural analysis of MOF-1040. In common with other crown-ether containing MOFs,[2] the polyether part of the coordinating ring were found to be highly disordered in the structure (see Supporting Information for details). In addition, the interstitial spaces of MOF-1040 are not only occupied by disordered solvent molecules, but also by disordered $\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{-}$ ions. The polyether loops of the coordinating rings in the structure were modeled by using Material Studio software.[10] Nonetheless, the crystal structure of MOF-1040 confirms that the integrity of the copper(I)-complexed [2]pseudorotaxanate is maintained after the formation of the MOF.
The stable $\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{I}}\mathrm{N}_{4}^{+}$ complex of MOF-1040 with fourcoordinate ${\tt d}^{10}$ metal (pseudo-tetrahedral) is oxidized (Figure 3a) by the addition of oxone solution in $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ (0.05 m) at room temperature, forming the paramagnetic copper(II) complex, $\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{N}_{4}^{2+}$ ( $\mathrm{^{\circ}d^{\circ}}$ configuration), in the oxidized MOF1040 (referred to as MOF-1041). The oxidation of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{I}}$ to $\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ has been monitored (Figure 3b) by EPR spectroscopy. While it is important to note that the EPR experiment does not allow us to quantify the degree of change in the redox state of copper ions, those ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{I}}$ ions which happen to be oxidized to $\mathrm{{Cu}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ can be regarded as local electronic switches which will undoubtedly be accompanied by significant[12] alterations in the coordination geometries surrounding these copper ions located inside MOF-1040/1041. The resulting spectrum displays characteristic features of a $\mathrm{d}^{9}\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{II}}N_{4}}^{+}$ complex with $g\mathrm{.}$ - factors of $g_{||}=2.290$ , $g_{\perp}=2.064$ and $A_{||}=11.1\mathrm{mT}$ , values expected for four-coordinate $\mathrm{{Cu}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ in a distorted tetrahedral arrangement. These values are in perfect agreement with those obtained[13] in the solution studies of related complexes consisting of the same chelating units, namely 2,9-diphenyl1,10-phenanthroline. Additionally, we have performed inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) in order to quantify the amount of copper within the framework.
MOF-1041 revealed a decrease in the amount of copper with the calculated molar ratios $[Z\mathrm{n}]/[\mathrm{Cu}]$ of 1.33 and 1.50, before and after oxidation, respectively. These numbers support the fact that the complex of the copper(II) ion in a fourcoordinate environment is a thermodynamically unstable species and can be dissociated easily.[13b,14] Furthermore, the powder $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern (Figure 3c) of MOF-1041 proves conclusively that the structure of the framework is maintained after oxidation has occurred.
In addition to the switching experiments, we have carried out demetalation (Figure 3) by following well-established literature procedures already developed for the molecular strut,[14] wherein ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{{I}}}$ -complexed rotaxanes and catenanes can be demetalated by reaction with $\mathbf{CN}^{-}$ ion under mild reaction conditions, leading to rotaxanes and catenanes with free coordination sites. In order to demetalate the rotaxane in MOF-1040, a reaction between MOF-1040 crystals and KCN $(5\times10^{-3}\mathbf{u})$ in $\mathrm{CH_{3}O H}$ has been performed (see Supporting Information for details). After the reaction, a sample analyzed by ICP-MS revealed a $60\%$ decrease in the amount of $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ , compared with the original sample, indicating that not all of the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{{I}}}$ -complexing sites within the framework are accessible.[15] The partially demetalated MOF-1040 (termed MOF-1042) maintains (Figure 3c) its structural topology throughout the reaction as evidenced by PXRD measurements.
We have demonstrated that metal-templated [2]pseudorotaxanates bearing carboxyl groups can be incorporated into metal–organic frameworks. The structural characterization of MOF-1040, based on $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray structural analysis, shows that these struts form threefold interpenetrated pcu networks. While both the oxidation and demetalation experiments have been demonstrated to occur without disrupting the crystallinity of the frameworks, the fact that at least some of the copper ions can be oxidized to their dicationic states indicates the presence of electronic switches which are presumably accompanied by geometrical changes involving shrinking and flattening in the coordination sphere of the copper ions in question. These observations allow us to contemplate incorporating molecular switches into the metal–organic frameworks, thus uniting the concepts of mechanostereochemistry,[16] chemical topology[17] and reticular chemistry[18] to create robust, yet dynamic systems.[1d] They mark a significant step towards transferring the solution-state chemistry of mechanically interlocked molecules into the solid-state in the form of a porous extended structure.
